WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.543
How should you check whether or not a file
or directory exists in Python?

00:00:04.543 --> 00:00:06.494
This is one of those 
things that's confusing

00:00:06.494 --> 00:00:09.720
mostly because Python 
offers several different ways to do it.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.980
So, which one should you use?

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.856
I've seen many beginners 
follow a path like this:

00:00:14.856 --> 00:00:19.679
First, they check to see if the path 
exists using something like `os.path.exists(file)`.

00:00:19.679 --> 00:00:21.631
Print something 
in return if it doesn't.

00:00:21.631 --> 00:00:25.884
And then proceed to open the file 
and do something with the data if it does.

00:00:25.884 --> 00:00:29.218
In most situations, although 
this does have some problems,

00:00:29.218 --> 00:00:32.237
let's be honest, it's  
probably fine.

00:00:32.237 --> 00:00:36.892
For small personal scripts and otherthings like that, 
I probably wouldn't even worry about it.

00:00:36.892 --> 00:00:40.132
But if your intention is to build
 something just a little bit more robust,

00:00:40.132 --> 00:00:42.253
then let's take a 
look at some of the issues.

00:00:42.253 --> 00:00:46.843
The first issue is that time passes
 between when we check to see if the file exists.

00:00:46.843 --> 00:00:48.990
And when we try to open it.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:52.979
It's possible that another thread or some
external process deleted the file

00:00:52.979 --> 00:00:56.556
between when we check whether 
it exists and when we try to open it.

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:59.029
If that were to happen, this open 
call would raise an exception,

00:00:59.029 --> 00:01:03.206
specifically a `FileNotFoundError`, 
which is something that you might like to catch.

00:01:03.206 --> 00:01:06.732
So really, we need to basically 
repeat our error handling code.

00:01:06.732 --> 00:01:09.604
Okay, so we surround 
it in a try-except.

00:01:09.604 --> 00:01:13.081
But wait, if `open()` was going to 
throw if the file didn't exist,

00:01:13.081 --> 00:01:16.569
then why did we need to
check if it exists in the first place?

00:01:16.569 --> 00:01:21.000
And that's my point - checking whether it
exists or not is basically redundant.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.532
If you're going to open the file immediately 
after checking whether it exists,

00:01:24.532 --> 00:01:27.136
instead, just try to open it.

00:01:27.136 --> 00:01:31.014
It either exists and opens 
successfully, or you get an exception.

00:01:31.014 --> 00:01:34.185
When you do it this way, the 
operating system will prevent other processes

00:01:34.185 --> 00:01:38.473
from deleting the file out from under 
you as long as you have it open in Python.

00:01:38.473 --> 00:01:43.588
Although it typically will not prevent the
 same process from deleting it out from under itself,

00:01:43.588 --> 00:01:47.466
it's still possible that other threads
 or processes read or write to the file

00:01:47.466 --> 00:01:51.372
in between when you 
open it and when you read it.

00:01:51.372 --> 00:01:55.563
So, there are still things you need to worry
about, just a few less of them.

00:01:55.563 --> 00:01:58.399
Another advantage of 
this approach is that there are other things

00:01:58.399 --> 00:02:02.331
that can go wrong when you try to 
open a file besides the file not being found.

00:02:02.331 --> 00:02:08.144
For instance, you might get an `IsADirectoryError` if 
you try to open a directory instead of a file.

00:02:08.144 --> 00:02:11.750
You could use the `os.path` function 
to check if something's a directory,

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:14.929
but once again, you'd end 
up with a potential very subtle bug.

00:02:14.929 --> 00:02:18.389
If whether it's a directory or not 
changes in between when you ask the question

00:02:18.389 --> 00:02:21.003
and when you actually 
try to open the file.

00:02:21.003 --> 00:02:24.844
So, if you're checking whether the 
file exists because you want to open it, then don't.

00:02:24.844 --> 00:02:28.404
Just try to open it and catch 
whatever errors you get.

00:02:28.404 --> 00:02:29.774
And if you want to be super modern,

00:02:29.774 --> 00:02:35.016
you might even consider using `pathlib.Path(file).read_text()` 
as a quick one-liner to get all the data out.

00:02:35.016 --> 00:02:37.589
But sometimes you don't 
want to actually open the file.

00:02:37.589 --> 00:02:41.130
You really do just want to 
know whether or not it exists.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.363
You could be using the 
existence of a file as a boolean.

00:02:44.363 --> 00:02:49.767
Checking whether an old temp file exists or 
checking if the user made a configuration directory.

00:02:49.767 --> 00:02:53.425
In that case, the recommended way to
 check whether or not a file or directory exists

00:02:53.425 --> 00:02:59.964
depends on whether you really need strings
or whether you could do with `pathlib` path objects.

00:02:59.964 --> 00:03:03.784
Many applications default to strings 
because it seems like the easiest choice.

00:03:03.784 --> 00:03:06.410
And in a lot of cases, it is.

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:09.953
If that's you, then `os.path.exists` 
might be what you want.

00:03:09.953 --> 00:03:14.161
Hopefully, unsurprisingly, it tells 
you whether or not the file exists.

00:03:14.161 --> 00:03:19.205
`exists` will return true for files and directories, 
and it also follows symbolic links.

00:03:19.205 --> 00:03:24.861
In particular, `exists` will return false for a 
symbolic link that points to a file that doesn't exist.

00:03:24.861 --> 00:03:30.746
So even if there is an actual file for the symbolic link 
at this location, it would still return false.

00:03:30.746 --> 00:03:34.662
It does sound a bit confusing, but this 
is the behavior that you would usually want.

00:03:34.662 --> 00:03:39.101
If you want to check that you got an actual file
and not a directory or some kind of device object,

00:03:39.101 --> 00:03:41.459
then you want `isfile()` instead.

00:03:41.459 --> 00:03:43.507
Once again, `isfile()` follows links.

00:03:43.507 --> 00:03:47.257
And this is probably what you want, 
even though it sounds a little confusing.

00:03:47.257 --> 00:03:48.223
And you guessed it.

00:03:48.223 --> 00:03:52.175
For directories, there's `isdir()`, and it
also follows symbolic links.

00:03:52.175 --> 00:03:54.865
So that's all fine 
and dandy, and it works.

00:03:54.865 --> 00:03:58.888
But using strings to represent 
paths can be kind of error-prone.

00:03:58.888 --> 00:04:04.875
Instead, using a `pathlib.Path()` can
 provide a more ergonomic and safer interface.

00:04:04.875 --> 00:04:09.624
If you already have a string, you can convert
it to a path very easily like this: `pathlib.Path(file)`.

00:04:09.624 --> 00:04:13.908
 Or you can do it like this if you're constructing
from scratch.

00:04:13.908 --> 00:04:17.685
`path` provides a wrapper 
around a lot of the `os.path` functionality.

00:04:17.685 --> 00:04:20.212
And they make it easy to 
access through method calls.

00:04:20.212 --> 00:04:23.921
For instance, `path.exists` will 
use `os.path.exists`,

00:04:23.921 --> 00:04:27.603
`path.is_file` will use `os.path.isfile`.

00:04:27.603 --> 00:04:32.866
and similarly for `is_dir` and a number 
of other `os.path` functions.

00:04:32.866 --> 00:04:36.895
If you're already using strings all over the place, 
of course, there's a bit of overhead to convert.

00:04:36.895 --> 00:04:42.299
But once you do, pretty much every built-in 
function that takes a string path also takes a `pathlib.Path`.

00:04:42.299 --> 00:04:45.729
And importantly, `pathlib.Path` 
takes care of a lot of idiosyncrasies

00:04:45.729 --> 00:04:48.584
that might come up if you 
were to switch operating systems.

00:04:48.584 --> 00:04:52.552
Things like using forward slashes
 on Linux but backslashes on Windows.

00:04:52.552 --> 00:04:59.399
Oh, and `pathlib` actually follows PEP 8.

